      "text": "   \nTo find the greatest common factor (GCF) of two numbers, I can use the Euclidean algorithm, which works by dividing the larger number by the smaller one until it yields a remainder of zero. Then I look at the quotient and repeat the process until I find a non-zero remainder. The last non-zero remainder is the GCF.\n\nQuestion 1: What are the first few steps that will enable me to find GCF of 40 and 48?\n\nStep 1. Start with the greater number (48) and divide by the smaller number (40). \n\nStep 2. Write down the divisibility of the smaller number (40) into the bigger one (48) and the remainder.\n\nStep 3. Adding the divisibility into (48), divide the remainder into it.\n\nGiving Back Step 1:\n\nStep 2:\n\n40 | 48\n  ≡    8\n Remainder = 8\n\nStep 3:\n\nGoing back to Step 2:\n\nStep 1 (GCF)\n\n40 | 48\n≡ 2\n8 |  2\n       ≡ 2\n                                          -   2\n              -  2\n              -  2\n      9 | 2\n                ≡ 0\n                                                          \nGCF of 40 & 48 is = 2. \n\nQuestion 2: How to find GCF of 48 and 80 if the first steps of question 1 is not useful for solving the new problem? \n   \nConsidering in the calculation of GCF method, when one number is greater than the other number then the first number is automatically considered as divisor 1 and hence greatest common divisor cannot be any number which is less than the product of both numbers.\nIn this case there is no way to use (GCF). Thus we use the Euclidean Algorithm to find GCF.",
